var callCount = 0;
_trackers: {
    createTracker: function() {
        callCount++;
        if (callCount === 1) {
            return { postComment: function(k, c) { postComments.push(c); }, removeLabel: function(){}, ... };
        }
        throw new Error('config tracker blew up');
    }
```
Then action: first createTracker(null, {}) → fake tracker. Inside try: loadProjectConfig (fine, defaults), then createTracker(config, ...) throws → catch: tracker.postComment(params.ticket.key, error comment) → recorded; removeLabel: smTriggerLabel null → skip; return {success:false, error:'Error: config tracker blew up'}. Assert result.success false, error contains 'config tracker blew up', postComments[0] contains '❌ Test Automation Error' and the message. This pins the catch branch.

Note the catch path also covers errors from configLoader.loadProjectConfig etc. This is a faithful characterization of the catch branch.

